Agent: Here's the stall mechanism. A clip with a comp boundary is resolved into parts. Each scheduling pass covers a window from the playhead to one look-ahead (100 ms) ahead, and a part is emitted only when its start falls inside that window. So the second part, the selected take that starts at the boundary, is emitted roughly 100 ms before it's due. If the main thread stalls for more than 100 ms right after Play (and on a hosted runner with throttled CPU, that's exactly what happens while the app warms up), the next pass runs after the boundary has passed. The part is then late: it joins mid-buffer with an offset, so the head of the take — usually the attack — is gone.

Cheapest fix: raise the look-ahead. At 500 ms a stall would need to be five times as long.

José: no. look-ahead is how late every live edit lands. fader automation, mutes, tempo, everything that's scheduled waits a look-ahead. 500 ms is a different product. and it only moves the threshold.

Agent: Agreed, it only moves the threshold. The structural fix: the boundary is known the moment the first part is emitted, so when a part is emitted, also emit the parts that continue it within the same clip, at their exact boundary instants. The audio graph then has the whole contiguous run scheduled sample-accurately, and a later stall can't make a boundary late.

José: yes. that.

