José: that's unusable. ok — not flush. only cut what changed. a source whose part the current comp resolution no longer contains gets cut; everything else keeps playing.

Agent: Understood, reversing the flush. New approach: each scheduling pass compares the already-emitted parts against the current comp resolution of their clips. Any emitted part the current resolution no longer contains (different take, different span, clip moved, trimmed, deleted or muted) is cut; parts still contained keep playing untouched; then the pass schedules the current parts that aren't emitted yet. No store subscription; the scheduler's own pass does it.

For the cut itself I'd add a dedicated 2 ms ramp so the switch is tighter than the teardown fade.

José: why a new ramp. there's a 5 ms teardown ramp already, it's click-free and every stop uses it. use that. one ramp.

