    1	# Boundary Enforcement — Laundering Patterns and How We Close Them
    2	
    3	A green `pnpm deps:validate` run is **not** proof the architecture is intact. Dependency-cruiser
    4	enforces resolved import **edges**; it does not understand re-export provenance, layering
    5	_direction_, or write-surface breadth, and by default it never sees type-only edges. This doc is the
    6	durable checklist of the ways a real boundary violation can slip past the tooling, and the concrete
    7	mechanism this repo uses to close each one.
    8	
    9	This complements the boundary model in
   10	[03-typescript-module.md](./03-typescript-module.md) (§3.3, §4, §6). When you add or change a
   11	boundary rule, check it against every row below.
   12	
   13	## The gates
   14	
   15	`pnpm deps:validate` (`scripts/check-dependency-boundaries.mjs`) first runs its static source
   16	guards, then six gates, each with its own exact known-violations baseline (repaired debt cannot stay
   17	silently authorized):
   18	
   19	| Gate                    | Source                                 | Sees                                                    |
   20	| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
   21	| main                    | `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`              | value + tagged type-only edges (`tsPreCompilationDeps`) |
   22	| contract re-export reg. | static `export … from` syntax reader   | re-exports of private folders from contract folders     |
   23	| module tiers            | `.dependency-cruiser.module-tiers.cjs` | module-pair edges against tiers; module-level cycles    |
   24	| reachability            | `.dependency-cruiser.reachability.cjs` | transitive causal edges                                 |
   25	| types                   | `.dependency-cruiser.types.cjs`        | type-only-specific duplicate boundary rules             |
   26	| tests                   | `.dependency-cruiser.tests.cjs`        | test-inclusive barrel boundaries                        |
   27	
   28	## The six laundering patterns
   29	
   30	1. **Type-only imports are invisible.** With `tsPreCompilationDeps` unset, `import type` edges do
   31	   not exist, so a module can import another module's private model/handler _types_ with zero
   32	   value-edge violations.
   33	   → **Closed by** the dedicated **types cruise** (`.dependency-cruiser.types.cjs`,
   34	   `tsPreCompilationDeps: 'specify'`), which re-runs the boundary rules against `type-only` edges as
   35	   `<rule>-type-only`, plus ESLint `sourdaw/no-type-only-private-module-import`. A deep type import
   36	   from `src/app`/`src/routes` trips `external-module-contracts-only-type-only`.
   37	
   38	2. **Re-export laundering through a barrel.** A legal contract barrel (or a use-case file) can
